  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256025/assets/3a0350d971fb1585e0bc6c8c81171d666d228d823cfbc1ed71542ae9a8fb8f30.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+C).$ What is the value of $C?$",
  "worked_solution": "The constant $C$ is the horizontal shift of the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256025/assets/ad3caf97c17668302e1c7dbc14aeff4455abac09b5febe3f413fa74f7b0eaac4.png)\n\nTo plot the given graph, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Vertically stretch it with scale factor $4$ to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units right to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore $C=-\\frac{3}{2}.$",
